<header><div class="eyebrow">Engineering decision record · 31 October 2026</div><h1>SpiceSharp: what to reuse,<br>what to verify, and what to retain</h1><p>An executable assessment of SpiceSharp 3.2.3 and SpiceSharpParser 3.4.1 against the current simulator direction. Rust and drop-in CPU solvers remain accepted choices; no production migration was performed.</p><span class="badge">Pinned NuGet packages</span><span class="badge">Windows · .NET 10</span><span class="badge">Independent ngspice checks</span><span class="badge">Known discrepancies preserved</span></header>
<main><nav aria-label="Report sections"><a href="#decision">Recommendation</a><a href="#maintenance">Development activity</a><a href="#measurements">Measured results</a><a href="#findings">Numerical findings</a><a href="#grammar">Parser evidence</a><a href="#architecture">Rust integration</a><a href="#verification">Verification limits</a><a href="#reproduce">Reproduce</a></nav>
<section id="decision" class="decision"><h2>Recommendation: retain Rust, reuse deliberately</h2>
<p><strong>Keep the Rust numerical core and its drop-in solver architecture.</strong> The user has explicitly affirmed that direction. SpiceSharp is nevertheless a useful independent reference and a serious source of reusable functionality. Its existence should have been evaluated before repeated custom parser and model expansion.</p>
<p><strong>Prioritize a real grammar/AST/elaboration boundary before more syntax exceptions.</strong> Evaluate SpiceSharpParser as an optional managed front end or differential reference. Its published BNF is useful, but this trial found observable-adapter behavior that cannot be accepted blindly.</p>
<p><strong>Do not treat SpiceSharp as a drop-in replacement for every ngspice circuit or for our linear-solver trait.</strong> It owns a complete analysis stack. A reference integration belongs above the analysis boundary, while existing linear providers remain independently selectable inside Rust.</p>
<div class="callout">No solver migration, numerical-source replacement, upstream patch, CI dispatch, or new endurance campaign was performed. The unfinished sheet-resistor experiment is preserved locally and excluded from this decision.</div>
<div class="cards"><div class="card"><strong>{{{states["parsed"]}}} / {{{corpus.Length}}}</strong><span>structurally accepted by parser-only trial</span></div><div class="card"><strong>{{{numericPass}}} / {{{numerical.Length}}}</strong><span>owned numerical groups within analytical bounds</span></div><div class="card"><strong>2 / 2</strong><span>owned malformed inputs explicitly rejected</span></div><div class="card"><strong>64</strong><span>seeded direct-engine divider cases passed</span></div></div>
<p class="muted">These counts measure different scopes. In particular, 488 parser acceptances must not be compared with the Rust corpus's one full end-to-end profile pass as if they were the same test.</p></section>
<section id="maintenance"><h2>Is it still under development?</h2><p><strong>Yes: there is 2026 maintenance activity. Engine releases are infrequent; parser activity is more recent.</strong> Neither repository was archived or disabled when checked. This is evidence of ongoing activity, not a guarantee of response time, future releases, or production fitness.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>Latest observed default-branch commit</th><th>Released package tested</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>SpiceSharp engine</th><td><a href="{{{E(engineHead.GetProperty("url").GetString())}}}">{{{E(engineHead.GetProperty("date").GetString())}}}</a><br>{{{E(engineHead.GetProperty("message").GetString())}}}</td><td>3.2.3<br><a href="https://github.com/SpiceSharp/SpiceSharp/releases/tag/v3.2.3">11 October 2024 release</a></td><td>May 2026 matrix-solver fix and June 2026 merge are recent code maintenance. Do not mistake later generator-tool releases for new engine releases.</td></tr>
<tr><th>SpiceSharpParser</th><td><a href="{{{E(parserHead.GetProperty("url").GetString())}}}">{{{E(parserHead.GetProperty("date").GetString())}}}</a><br>{{{E(parserHead.GetProperty("message").GetString())}}}</td><td>3.4.1<br><a href="https://github.com/SpiceSharp/SpiceSharpParser/releases/tag/3.4.1">26 July 2026 release</a></td><td>Recent activity includes documentation. That alone is not evidence of recent fixes to each numerical/export path.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>Checked UTC: <code>{{{E(activity.RootElement.GetProperty("checked_utc").GetString())}}}</code>. <a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/activity.json">Exact GitHub API snapshot</a>. Package versions were also checked against NuGet and locked locally.</p></section>
<section id="measurements"><h2>What actually ran</h2><p>The isolated assessment used released packages, not a source fork. Nine original numerical case groups exercised OP, complex AC, transient, subcircuit/parameter expansion, multiple analyses, a diode, a BJT and a level-1 MOSFET. Two malformed cases tested rejection. Named voltages and signed currents were checked against independent equations; executed cases were then compared with ngspice-47+.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Owned fixture</th><th>Analytical disposition</th><th>Observed scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{{summaries}}}</tbody></table></div>
<p>The independent ngspice run agreed within declared print-table bounds for <strong>9 of 11 analysis instances</strong>; the two retained disagreements are the RL real-current export and the diode constant convention. The multiple-analysis fixture contains three analysis instances. Negative fixtures are not numerical passes.</p>
<p>Candidate campaign wall time: <strong>{{{F(candidate.RootElement.GetProperty("elapsed_seconds").GetDouble())}}} seconds</strong>, including worker startup, file hashing and orchestration. This is <strong>not</strong> a solver speed comparison. All measured cases are small; no large sparse matrix, difficult transistor convergence or scaling benchmark was run.</p></section>
<section id="findings"><h2>Two disagreements, with different causes</h2>
<h3>1. Real AC current: parser/export adapter discrepancy</h3><p>For the 1 kΩ + 1 H series circuit at 100 Hz, the independent current is <code>1 / (1000 + j·2π·100)</code>. The parser's <code>IR(L1)</code> export returned <strong>0 A</strong>, while its imaginary export was correct.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Real current (A)</th><th>Imaginary current (A)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Independent equation</td><td>{{{E(rl.GetProperty("expected_real"))}}}</td><td>{{{E(rl.GetProperty("expected_imag"))}}}</td></tr><tr><td>Direct engine ComplexCurrentExport</td><td>{{{E(rl.GetProperty("actual_real"))}}}</td><td>{{{E(rl.GetProperty("actual_imag"))}}}</td></tr><tr><td>Parser IR / II exports</td><td>0 — outside bound</td><td>−0.0004504772433683887 — agrees</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The exact released parser source constructs <code>CurrentRealExport</code> using <code>RealPropertyExport</code>, whereas the imaginary path uses <code>ComplexPropertyExport</code>. Together with the direct-engine counterexample, this points to the adapter, not the AC solve. <a href="https://github.com/SpiceSharp/SpiceSharpParser/blob/a901846b0a5665a754bc9e0a371ea559b0f464ee/src/SpiceSharpParser/ModelReaders/Netlist/Spice/Readers/Controls/Exporters/CurrentExports/CurrentRealExport.cs">Pinned exporter source</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Disposition:</strong> keep this failure; bypass or fix and qualify the adapter before relying on it. No upstream patch or issue was submitted, and no candidate code was changed.</p>
<h3>2. Diode current: legacy physical constants explain the difference</h3><p>At 0.6 V, IS = 10⁻¹² A and 300.15 K, the candidate gave <code>{{{E(diode.GetProperty("actual"))}}} A</code>. The analytical target using current SI constants was <code>{{{E(diode.GetProperty("Value"))}}} A</code>, a <strong>{{{drift.ToString("0.0000",CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}}}%</strong> difference. Ngspice also disagreed under the original declared bound.</p>
<p>The installed engine exposes Boltzmann = <code>1.3806226e−23</code> and charge = <code>1.6021918e−19</code>, rather than the current SI values used by the analytical target. Using the engine's exposed constants predicts <code>{{{E(direct.RootElement.GetProperty("diode_using_library_constants"))}}} A</code>, matching its result to about 6×10⁻¹³ A.</p>
<p><strong>Disposition:</strong> this is an explained compatibility convention, not proof of a general solver defect. The original failure remains a failure under its original acceptance bounds. Do not silently change constants, model parameters or tolerances; choose and document the convention if integrating it.</p>
<h3>Reference-harness correction</h3><p>The first ngspice batch attempt combined OP/AC/transient print tables in one deck and produced unavailable/interleaved output. That was an assessment-harness limitation, not a SpiceSharp numerical failure. The corrected reference helper executes every declared owned analysis in a separate oracle deck with unchanged circuit equations. Both the initial and corrected reference reports are retained.</p></section>
<section id="grammar"><h2>Parser-only corpus: useful coverage, substantial gaps</h2><p>All 910 previously inventoried upstream decks were attempted in bounded workers. Every parser preprocessor was disabled: no include/library resolution, subcircuit expansion, parameter elaboration, control execution or numerical reader was invoked. Default parser settings were otherwise retained. The result is <strong>structural syntax acceptance by this parser</strong>, not proof that every ngspice-required field or ending was validated.</p>{{{bars}}}
<p>The two timed-out files were <code>examples/klu/Circuits/85/c2670/c2670_ann.net</code> and <code>examples/klu/Circuits/85/c3540/c3540_ann.net</code>. The three-second limit includes .NET worker startup and parsing. Timeouts are unavailable evidence, not rejected grammar or passes. No corpus rows were left budget-unattempted.</p>
<p>Rejected decks may contain valid ngspice-specific constructs, malformed source, unsupported lexical forms or different contextual rules. This assessment does not adjudicate all 409 rejections. The parser has a published BNF and source-location facilities, but it still needs a pinned dialect conformance matrix before becoming our general front end.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/SpiceSharp/SpiceSharpParser/blob/main/src/SpiceSharpParser/Parsers/Netlist/Spice/SpiceGrammarBNF.txt">Published BNF</a> · <a href="https://github.com/SpiceSharp/SpiceSharpParser/blob/main/src/SpiceSharpParser/Parsers/Netlist/Spice/Internals/ParseTreeGenerator.cs">Handwritten LL(*) implementation</a>. It uses a custom regex lexer and handwritten parser, not Parlot. Its generic component/directive productions do not themselves prove device-parameter semantics.</p></section>
<section id="architecture"><h2>How it fits the Rust and drop-in solver direction</h2><div class="flow"><b>SPICE syntax / AST</b><span>→</span><b>Elaboration + capability checks</b><span>→</span><b>Rust analysis engine</b><span>→</span><b>Chosen linear provider</b></div>
<table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Value</th><th>Costs / boundaries</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Retain Rust + current solver adapters</th><td>Preserves numerical control, sparse-provider experiments, contracts and existing qualification investment.</td><td>Still needs the grammar/AST boundary. No measured performance advantage over SpiceSharp was established here.</td><td>Keep, as explicitly directed by the user.</td></tr>
<tr><th>SpiceSharpParser as optional .NET front end</th><td>Existing BNF, syntax tree, hierarchy and parameter machinery; natural fit with C#/F# hosts.</td><td>Shipping .NET dependency for that path; neutral AST/IR bridge; explicit dependency policy; ngspice gaps and adapter bugs need qualification.</td><td>Bounded integration candidate, not an automatic adoption.</td></tr>
<tr><th>SpiceSharp as independent analysis reference</th><td>Actual BJT, level-1 MOS, RC transient and complex AC execution in this trial.</td><td>Owns Newton/integration/model behavior; is not merely an implementation of our linear-solver trait. Use an analysis-level adapter with truthful provider metadata.</td><td>Good reference candidate using direct APIs for affected observables.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Ngspice integration / external oracle</th><td>Already available; provides a broader reference for its own syntax and model ecosystem.</td><td>Native packaging, licensing of exact build/options, result adapter and execution/resource policy still matter.</td><td>Retain independent oracle; evaluate optional full-engine integration against specific needs.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Reimplement its parser or semiconductor models in Rust</th><td>Could avoid a managed runtime dependency.</td><td>Duplicates mature work and creates substantial conformance/numerical maintenance cost.</td><td>Not justified solely by language preference. Require a concrete gap or measured advantage.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Model breadth is not universal compatibility</h3><p>The pinned engine's exported component inventory includes diode, bipolar, JFET and MOS levels 1–3. This trial numerically checks only one NPN and one level-1 NMOS configuration, not every model. A separate level-54 BSIM4 probe was explicitly rejected with <code>Unknown mosfet model level 54</code>. No OSDI integration was demonstrated; optional custom-component packages were not evaluated.</p>
<h3>What existing work can still contribute</h3><ul><li>Typed request/result contracts, reproducible evidence, failure classification and remote campaign plumbing.</li><li>Independent analytical cases, ngspice comparisons, physical audits and retained mutation counterexamples.</li><li>Rust numerical experiments and qualified solver slices where they meet an actual requirement.</li></ul><p>These are candidates for retention, not a valuation of all prior expenditure. This trial does not establish how much migration or maintenance work each option would cost.</p></section>
<section id="verification"><h2>Verification, licenses and practical limits</h2><p>SpiceSharp 3.2.3 and SpiceSharpParser 3.4.1 declare MIT licensing. The parser also brings SpiceSharpBehavioral 3.2.0 and Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp 4.12.0 with transitive dependencies. Lockfiles and package metadata are retained. No third-party source or binaries are vendored by this assessment; final distribution still needs the actual notices and dependency review.</p>
<p>The new direct-engine FsCheck property passed <strong>64 generated divider cases</strong>, replay <code>192,20261031</code>, with varied voltage signs and resistor ratios. The trial also included two malformed netlists and explicit known counterexamples. No upstream mutation campaign, upstream full test suite, coverage-guided fuzz campaign or new soak was executed. Observed discrepancy detection is not a manufactured mutation score.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Owned assessment source</th><th>Covered lines</th><th>Covered branches</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{{coverageRows}}}</tbody></table>
<p>These are scoped <code>dotnet-coverage 18.12</code> measurements of the assessment glue, not SpiceSharp/SpiceSharpParser internal coverage. A separate Coverlet measurement maps the owned property assertion body. Some worker failure paths remain unmeasured. <a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/report-coverage.json">Report-generator coverage</a> is retained separately. Coverage does not establish the correctness of the adopted library or justify the build-versus-reuse decision.</p>
<p><strong>Unavailable:</strong> fresh Linux/macOS execution, NativeAOT qualification, GPU behavior, difficult modern semiconductor convergence, large-circuit scaling, comparative solver performance and full ngspice compatibility. Reflection and Roslyn dependencies warrant an AOT/deployment investigation if that becomes a requirement; no AOT failure or success is asserted here.</p>
<p>Requirements <code>REUSE-001</code>/<code>REUSE-002</code> and <code>NGC-PARSE-001…004</code> remain Draft. The bounded assessment supports a decision; it does not close the project's M1 or production release gates.</p></section>
<section id="next"><h2>Next bounded decisions</h2><ol><li>Keep Rust and current linear-provider selection. Do not resume card-by-card syntax growth as the parser strategy.</li><li>Define the pinned ngspice grammar and source-located AST contract; separate syntactic acceptance, elaboration and supported numerical capability.</li><li>Trial a small neutral-IR bridge or grammar-reference comparison on representative includes, subcircuits, expressions and multiple analyses. Decide whether the managed dependency is acceptable for that path before broad implementation.</li><li>If adding a SpiceSharp reference adapter, use direct complex-current exports or qualify a corrected adapter; document physical constants and model conventions.</li><li>Choose further semiconductor/OSDI work from demonstrated requirements and actual library gaps, not an assumption that every component must be written again.</li></ol>
<div class="callout">The canonical personal guidance now requires a documented reuse-before-build assessment and a bounded executable trial before substantial replacement work. It explicitly rejects sunk cost, language preference alone and high test counts as sufficient justification.</div></section>
<section id="reproduce"><h2>Reproduction and evidence</h2><p>The assessment source is under <code>evaluation/</code>; the report generator is <code>scripts/RenderSpiceSharpAssessment.cs</code>. See <a href="../spicesharp-evaluation.md">setup and exact reproduction commands</a>. Production simulator dependencies and numerical code were not migrated.</p><ul>
<li><a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/candidate.json">Bounded candidate / corpus results</a> — 910 rows, owned analytical checks, first/last samples.</li><li><a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/oracle.json">Corrected independent oracle checks</a> and <a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/oracle-initial.json">initial reference attempt</a>.</li><li><a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/direct.json">Direct engine probe and physical constants</a>; <a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/bsim4.json">level-54 rejection</a>.</li><li><a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/activity.json">Development activity snapshot</a>; <a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/property.json">seeded property record</a>.</li><li><a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/helper-coverage.json">Scoped helper coverage</a>; <a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/property-coverage.json">property-code coverage</a>.</li><li><a href="../../evidence/spicesharp-assessment/manifest.json">Provenance and bounded projection manifest</a>.</li></ul><p>Raw files, full waveforms and local logs stay under the ignored <code>work/</code> directory. The repository artifacts are bounded evidence. Package binaries are restored through NuGet; they are not committed. Report generation and the trial used managed .NET execution.</p></section>
</main><footer>Assessment, not a production qualification certificate. All source-inspection claims, actual executions and unresolved findings are distinguished. This offline HTML makes no external network requests.</footer></body></html>
""";
File.WriteAllText(output,html,new UTF8Encoding(false));
Console.WriteLine($"Wrote {output} ({html.Length} characters)");
